From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] d3d9umd: one device call at a time

With batching, the Direct3D 9 runtime calls LockAsync and UnlockAsync from the application's thread while its worker
thread makes the other device calls, and nine isn't thread safe: an unlock adding its buffer to the device's list of
buffers to upload while a draw walked that list left the walk on an unlinked node, calling NineBuffer9_Upload on it
forever (3DMark06 froze, a core busy in NineBuffer9_RebindIfRequired). Every device entry point now holds one
recursive mutex.
---
diff --git a/src/gallium/frontends/d3d9umd/d3d9umd.h b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d9umd/d3d9umd.h
index 38044f4..baecc17 100644
--- a/src/gallium/frontends/d3d9umd/d3d9umd.h
+++ b/src/gallium/frontends/d3d9umd/d3d9umd.h
@@ -84,6 +84,19 @@ static inline D3D9UmdResource *D3D9UmdCastResource(HANDLE h) { return (D3D9UmdRe
 void D3D9UmdLog(const char *format, ...);
 bool D3D9UmdTracing(void);
 void D3D9UmdTraceLog(const char *format, ...);
+/* The runtime makes LockAsync and UnlockAsync calls from the application's thread while its worker thread makes the
+ * others, and nine isn't thread safe (a buffer unlocked during a draw's walk of the managed buffers to upload left
+ * that walk looping on an unlinked node: 3DMark06 froze). Every device call holds this, recursively. */
+#include <mutex>
+static inline std::recursive_mutex &D3D9UmdDeviceMutex()
+{
+   static std::recursive_mutex mutex;
+   return mutex;
+}
+struct D3D9UmdDeviceLock {
+   std::lock_guard<std::recursive_mutex> guard{D3D9UmdDeviceMutex()};
+};
+
 #define D3D9UMD_TRACE(...) do { if (D3D9UmdTracing()) D3D9UmdTraceLog(__VA_ARGS__); } while (0)
 
 /* For DDI entry points that aren't implemented yet: logs the first call of each and returns S_OK */
